[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of 'objects', which contain data in the form of fields (often known as attributes or properties), and code, in the form of procedures (often known as methods)."},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java is an object-oriented programming language. This tutorial will cover the four fundamental principles of OOP: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction."},{"order":4,"type":"heading","text":"1. Encapsulation"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Encapsulation is the bundling of data (attributes) and methods that operate on that data into a single unit, or class. It also involves hiding the internal state of an object and requiring all interaction to be performed through an object's methods. This is achieved through access modifiers like 'private', 'protected', and 'public'."},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Benefits of Encapsulation:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Hiding: Prevents direct access to internal data, protecting it from accidental modification."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modularity: Code is organized into self-contained units, making it easier to understand and maintain."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Encapsulated classes can be reused in different parts of the application."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class BankAccount {
    private String accountNumber;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String accountNumber, double initialBalance) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {
            balance -= amount;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Insufficient balance or invalid amount.");
        }
    }

    public String getAccountNumber() {
        return accountNumber;
    }
}
"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'accountNumber' and 'balance' are private attributes, meaning they can only be accessed from within the BankAccount class. The methods 'getBalance', 'deposit', and 'withdraw' provide controlled access to the account's data."},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"2. Inheritance"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is a mechanism where a new class (subclass or derived class) inherits properties and behaviors from an existing class (superclass or base class). It promotes code reusability and establishes a 'is-a' relationship between classes."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java supports single inheritance (a class can inherit from only one superclass) but allows multiple levels of inheritance (a class can inherit from another class that inherits from another class, and so on)."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name); // Call the constructor of the superclass
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog myDog = new Dog("Buddy", "Golden Retriever");
        myDog.eat(); // Inherited from Animal class
        myDog.bark(); // Defined in Dog class
        System.out.println(myDog.name); // Accessing inherited attribute
    }
}
"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the 'Dog' class inherits from the 'Animal' class. It inherits the 'name' attribute and the 'eat()' method. The 'Dog' class also has its own attribute 'breed' and its own method 'bark()'."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"3. Polymorphism"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism means 'many forms'. In OOP, it refers to the ability of an object to take on many forms. More specifically, polymorphism allows objects of different classes to respond to the same method call in their own way."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java supports two types of polymorphism:"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Compile-time polymorphism (Method Overloading): Achieved by defining multiple methods in the same class with the same name but different parameters."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Runtime polymorphism (Method Overriding): Achieved by defining a method in a subclass that has the same signature (name and parameters) as a method in its superclass."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Overloading"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"class Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator calc = new Calculator();
        System.out.println(calc.add(2, 3)); // Calls the first add method
        System.out.println(calc.add(2.5, 3.5)); // Calls the second add method
        System.out.println(calc.add(2, 3, 4)); // Calls the third add method
    }
}
"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Calculator' class has three 'add' methods with different parameters. The compiler determines which method to call based on the arguments passed."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Overriding"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Generic animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Animal();
        Animal dog = new Dog();
        Animal cat = new Cat();

        animal.makeSound(); // Prints "Generic animal sound"
        dog.makeSound();    // Prints "Woof!"
        cat.makeSound();    // Prints "Meow!"
    }
}
"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Dog' and 'Cat' classes override the 'makeSound()' method of the 'Animal' class. When 'makeSound()' is called on a 'Dog' object, it prints 'Woof!', and when called on a 'Cat' object, it prints 'Meow!'. This demonstrates runtime polymorphism, where the actual method called is determined at runtime based on the object's type."},{"order":28,"type":"heading","text":"4. Abstraction"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Abstraction is the process of hiding complex implementation details and exposing only the essential features of an object. It allows you to focus on what an object does rather than how it does it."},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java, abstraction can be achieved using abstract classes and interfaces."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Abstract Classes"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly. It can contain abstract methods (methods without implementation) as well as concrete methods (methods with implementation). Subclasses of an abstract class must provide implementations for all abstract methods."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Shape {
    String color;

    // Abstract method
    abstract double area();

    // Concrete method
    public void displayColor() {
        System.out.println("Color: " + color);
    }

    // Constructor
    public Shape(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;

    public Circle(String color, double radius) {
        super(color);
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Circle circle = new Circle("Red", 5.0);
        circle.displayColor();
        System.out.println("Area: " + circle.area());
    }
}
"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Shape' class is abstract and has an abstract method 'area()'. The 'Circle' class extends 'Shape' and provides an implementation for 'area()'. You cannot create an instance of 'Shape' directly, but you can create an instance of 'Circle'."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Interfaces"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"An interface is a completely abstract class. It contains only abstract methods (implicitly public and abstract) and constant variables (implicitly public, static, and final). A class can implement multiple interfaces."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"interface Drawable {
    void draw();
}

interface Resizable {
    void resize(int width, int height);
}

class Rectangle implements Drawable, Resizable {
    int width, height;

    public Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing rectangle with width " + width + " and height " + height);
    }

    @Override
    public void resize(int newWidth, int newHeight) {
        this.width = newWidth;
        this.height = newHeight;
        System.out.println("Resizing rectangle to width " + width + " and height " + height);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rectangle rect = new Rectangle(10, 5);
        rect.draw();
        rect.resize(20, 10);
    }
}
"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'Rectangle' class implements both the 'Drawable' and 'Resizable' interfaces. It must provide implementations for the 'draw()' and 'resize()' methods defined in the interfaces."},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction are the core principles of Object-Oriented Programming. Understanding and applying these principles leads to writing more modular, reusable, and maintainable code."}]
